kvmalloc stage-1 host contract selftest
======================================

Run from any directory:
  python3 -I /home/ssx/android-kernel/tools/testing/selftests/kvmalloc/run.py
Or use python3 -I with the absolute path of run.py in another checkout.
Requires Python 3 and a host C compiler with ASan/UBSan (CC may override cc).
No kernel build, device, root, fetch, or network is needed. Generated C and
binaries live in an automatically removed temporary directory.

run.py extracts the CURRENT C definitions of kvmalloc, kvzalloc, kvfree from
mm/util.c and kvmalloc_array/kvcalloc from include/linux/mm.h. It also extracts
GFP constants/dependencies and PAGE_ALLOC_COSTLY_ORDER from this checkout.
It fails on missing/nonunique definitions or duplicate helpers in slab.h.
The bodies are not reimplemented in the test. test.c supplies deterministic
kmalloc/__vmalloc/free stubs and checks arguments, results and ownership.

Normal and ASan+UBSan runs cover simulated 4 KiB and 64 KiB PAGE_SIZE:
- zero, subpage, page, page+1, costly threshold and threshold+1 requests;
- GFP_KERNEL, ACCOUNT, ZERO, REPEAT, NOWARN and incompatible NOFS/NOIO,
  ATOMIC/NOWAIT/zero masks, including all success/failure combinations;
- kmalloc fast-fail flags versus unchanged vmalloc flags;
- kvzalloc/kvcalloc zeroing on both backends, and nonzero fill for kvmalloc;
- overflowing products rejected before either backend, maximum valid products
  forwarded without wrap, zero dimensions, array allocation and failure;
- unchanged kvfree routing for both backends, NULL and ZERO_SIZE_PTR.
Sanitizer errors, leaks, assertions and compiler warnings fail the run.

Limits
------
This is extracted-function host testing, not kernel-header compilation, KUnit,
boot testing, allocator stress or evidence of performance improvement. Page
size is simulated. It does NOT exercise actual reclaim, compaction, OOM,
memcg, NUMA, vmalloc page tables, concurrency, fault injection infrastructure,
or real failure warnings. ASan tests only the host harness. NOFAIL/NORETRY
are not supported caller contracts for fallback; no local global-helper
caller was found requesting them. GFP_KERNEL-superset is the upstream reclaim
compatibility test, not a guarantee for arbitrary zone/contiguity modifiers.
No NUMA helper API, HIGHMEM policy or vmalloc implementation is backported.

Source/equivalence audit (local git objects only, no fetch)
--------------------------------------------------------
Baseline HEAD: 0943f923a568938a85d40452df4ac36aa7e02fb6.
Local global helpers were introduced by 9d3f977c86f4 (Sony performance squash),
not by BakaSU. They unconditionally fell back after kmalloc/kzalloc failure.

rangga-sdm845/BakaSU-SUSFS:
  ff9e458bb6e842e32bb651da4c061d5f1f00841b
  No global helpers in mm/util.c or mm.h/slab.h. F2FS-private kvmalloc in
  fs/f2fs/f2fs.h, introduced by 357eb774b25e, only suppresses kmalloc warnings
  and prevents <=PAGE_SIZE fallback. It lacks fast-fail and GFP compatibility.

sm8250-mod/lunar-release:
  b13875fe8d80a133048954e7459931c54eb46929
  mm/util.c implements kvmalloc_node; mm.h wraps it and defines array helpers.
  Its __GFP_RETRY_MAYFAIL policy cannot be copied literally to this 4.9 tree.

Adapted source policies, verified with git show/log:
  a7c3e901a46f: original kv[mz]alloc helpers and <=PAGE_SIZE boundary.
  6c5ab6511f71: 4.9-style REPEAT/NORETRY costly-order policy (used here).
  ce91f6ee5b3b: GFP_KERNEL-incompatible requests use kmalloc only (used here).
  3b3b1a29eb89: array overflow check conversion in lunar. This patch uses
                the preceding division guard, matching local kmalloc_array;
                equivalent for size_t, without new include dependencies.
  1c542f38ab8d: kvcalloc wrapper policy; ZERO is passed through kvmalloc_array.
Existing global exported kvmalloc/kvzalloc ABI and kvfree are retained.
include/linux/slab.h is audited/backed up but intentionally unchanged.

Local callers/flags audit
------------------------
All direct global-helper callsites in mm/swap_state.c, swapfile.c,
swap_slots.c, crypto/lzo-rle.c, drivers/block/zram/zram_drv.c,
net/xfrm/xfrm_compat.c and fs/f2fs/extm.c use GFP_KERNEL, optionally ZERO.
F2FS wrappers in f2fs.h pass flags through. All f2fs_kvmalloc/kvzalloc callers
in file.c, node.c and segment.c use GFP_KERNEL. f2fs_kmalloc/kzalloc callers
also use GFP_NOFS and GFP_F2FS_ZERO (= NOFS | ZERO) in acl.c, data.c,
inline.c and xattr.c. Those must now remain kmalloc-only on failure; ENOMEM
paths already exist. The wrapper's initial kmalloc and existing free-site
contracts are not rewritten by this patch (large GFP_KERNEL fallback in that
kmalloc-named wrapper remains an independent pre-existing audit concern).

WireGuard compat.h shadows both names on 4.9 with its own static helpers;
peerlookup, ratelimiter and compat ptr_ring therefore do not use the changed
global body. queueing.c supplies GFP_KERNEL to its ptr_ring. Its private
fallback lacks the global GFP guard and is explicitly outside this patch.
KernelSU kernel_compat.c's __kvmalloc uses a separate <4.12 branch on 4.9;
its global call is in the inactive >=4.12 branch. sepolicy uses GFP_KERNEL.
AppArmor, ext4, KVM and ceph similarly named helpers are distinct APIs, not
calls to these global definitions. No existing kvmalloc_array/kvcalloc
function definition was found in the local tree before adding these helpers.

Scope excludes all traditional LRU/shadow/unevictable policies, LZ4KDR,
MGLRU switches, wholesale vmalloc changes, seq/BPF migrations and callers.
